\title{Tuple values and destructuring}
\taxon{Design}
\date{2026-10-14}
\author{schrodingerzy}
\tag{design}
\meta{created}{2026-10-14}
\meta{updated}{2026-10-14}
\meta{origin}{docs/design/tuples.md}
\import{base-macros}

\p{A tuple is an ordered, structurally typed, inline aggregate. It groups values without declaring a named record or allocating a new reference-counted box. Its field types, order, and arity determine its type. Fields may themselves be tuples, records, arrays, cells, or closures, subject to their existing storage and capability rules.}

\subtree[design-tuples-constructing-and-returning-tuples]{
\title{Constructing and returning tuples}

\pre{\verb~|
fn measurements(n: u64) -> (u64, (u64,), ()) {
    (n, (n + 1,), ())
}

pub fn total(n: u64) -> u64 {
    let (first, (second,), ()) = measurements(n);
    first + second
}
~}

\p{\code{\verb~|()~} is the existing unit value and type. \code{\verb~|(x)~} groups an expression, while \code{\verb~|(x,)~} constructs a one-field tuple. The same comma distinction applies to \code{\verb~|(T)~} and \code{\verb~|(T,)~} in type positions. A tuple can contain unit-valued fields; those fields retain their source indices even though they occupy no useful payload space. Numeric projections start at zero: \code{\verb~|pair.0~}, \code{\verb~|pair.1~}, and \code{\verb~|nested.1.0~}. Tuple fields are immutable; replace a tuple value rather than assigning through a position.}

\p{Tuple elements evaluate in source order. A destructuring \code{\verb~|let~} evaluates its right-hand side once, then binds the selected fields. Patterns can nest and use \code{\verb~|_~} to ignore a field. A single annotation describes the complete pattern:}

\pre{\verb~|
let (first, (second, _)): (u64, (bool, u64)) = (7, (true, 9));
~}

\p{Bindings in one destructuring pattern must have distinct names. A \code{\verb~|let~} pattern must be irrefutable; use \code{\verb~|match~} for literal and variant patterns.}
}

\subtree[design-tuples-closures-and-function-parameters]{
\title{Closures and function parameters}

\p{Function parameter declarations remain explicitly named. A tuple parameter is written \code{\verb~|fn consume(pair: (u64, u64))~}. Closure arrow types preserve their existing parameter-list syntax:}

\pre{\verb~|
fn apply_two(f: (u64, u64) -> u64) -> u64 {
    f(2, 3)
}

fn apply_pair(f: ((u64, u64),) -> u64) -> u64 {
    f((2, 3))
}
~}

\p{The first closure takes two arguments. The second takes one tuple argument. Tuples can be captured, passed through partial application, and returned by closures. Captured tuples follow ordinary closure cloning and ownership rules. Unit parameters still count toward source arity and partial application, but occupy no physical function or closure argument slot. Their argument expressions still execute once in source order, including effects before and after a unit argument.}
}

\subtree[design-tuples-matching]{
\title{Matching}

\p{Tuple patterns compose with literals, variant patterns, nested tuples, guards, and wildcards. Coverage is checked across the complete product of fields.}

\pre{\verb~|
fn classify(pair: (bool, bool)) -> u64 {
    match pair {
        (true, _) => 1,
        (_, true) => 2,
        (false, false) => 3
    }
}
~}

\p{Removing the last arm leaves a non-exhaustive match. Guards do not establish unconditional coverage: after a failed guard, matching continues with later arms against the same scrutinee. Field bindings retain the normal ownership rules throughout that retry.}
}

\subtree[design-tuples-ownership-and-storage]{
\title{Ownership and storage}

\p{Tuples use the existing value-aggregate representation. Acquiring or dropping a tuple recursively acquires or drops its managed fields. Copying a tuple that contains a shared record keeps the record alive; discarding a field releases its owned reference. Projecting one field from a temporary releases its other fields. A tuple itself adds no RC allocation.}

\p{A tuple can be a field of a shared or value record, and can contain either record kind. Inline recursive layouts remain invalid, including cycles hidden inside tuples, such as a value record with a \code{\verb~|(Self,)~} field. A boxed link breaks such a cycle. Tuple structural \code{\verb~|Sync~} checks each element using value-aggregate rules; surrounding atomic coloring does not implicitly promote tuple fields.}

\p{\code{\verb~|Cell<T>~} and \code{\verb~|RefCell<T>~} can store tuples. Reads acquire managed fields, replacement drops the previous payload, and a loaded tuple may outlive the cell. Tuples may also contain arrays, and an array fold can use a tuple accumulator. The existing array element policy still excludes inline tuple \strong{elements}. \code{\verb~|Atomic<T>~} requires a supported primitive; lock-guarded cells require a supported scalar or pointer slot. A tuple itself is neither an \code{\verb~|Arc~} payload box nor a nullable pointer.}

\p{Unfrozen regional values cannot escape into tuple storage. Kernel-only SSA values retain their scope restrictions when nested in tuples. These rules do not introduce new region or kernel execution operations.}

\p{Native C trampolines use the established packed-aggregate argument and return ABI. Polymorphic Rust FFI signatures currently reject inline tuple arguments and results, as they do value records; a tuple does not acquire a new Rust ABI.}
}

\subtree[design-tuples-serialization-and-examples]{
\title{Serialization and examples}

\p{Tuple types and expressions survive HIR and MIR textual round trips. Package interfaces preserve tuple fields and generic tuple bodies, allowing a consumer to instantiate imported functions without reconstructing a nominal record.}

\p{The executable examples are also regression tests:}

\ul{
\li{\<html:a>[href]{\verb~|https://github.com/reussir-lang/reussir/blob/main/tests/integration/frontend/tuple_values_e2e.rr~}{tuple_values_e2e.rr} demonstrates returned, singleton, nested, and unit tuples; evaluation order; managed ownership; record fields; cells; closures; arrays; tuple matches; and native aggregate passing, at ordinary and aggressive optimization.}
\li{\<html:a>[href]{\verb~|https://github.com/reussir-lang/reussir/blob/main/tests/integration/frontend/tuple_import.rr~}{tuple_import.rr} exercises a separately serialized generic dependency and imported managed records.}
\li{\<html:a>[href]{\verb~|https://github.com/reussir-lang/reussir/blob/main/tests/integration/frontend/tuple_patterns.rr~}{tuple_patterns.rr} checks exhaustiveness, guards, unreachable arms, arity, and duplicate bindings.}
\li{The \code{\verb~|tuple_*_error*.rr~} fixtures check storage constraints, generic bounds, inline recursive layouts, and the polymorphic FFI boundary.}
\li{\<html:a>[href]{\verb~|https://github.com/reussir-lang/reussir/blob/main/tests/integration/repl-rs/tuple_values.repl~}{tuple_values.repl} demonstrates structural display, persistent destructuring, typed bindings, recovery after invalid patterns, and managed values across rebinding in the REPL.}
}
}
